{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792947372,"nonce":"7BW9h51V52yeZOrh/QRi9hAhbNM=","message":"Plan changed (2026-10-25): no custom page agent is needed for the socket. Ash's browser host implements the client half of sys.net.Socket over WebSocket (ash docs/wasm/host-abi.md: ash_host_socket_connect/send/recv/poll; whole WebSocket messages are queued and drained by byte count, so message boundaries are lost at the Socket API), and ash-future's Future.await() parks a fiber while the page completes a browser promise (for fetch and anything else that is a promise). So the page talks to the session with sys.net.Socket and frames.\n\nDone (commit after a95fd47): the session protocol as length-prefixed frames (4 bytes big-endian, then UTF-8 JSON) over a raw TCP port (`--raw-port`) and as WebSocket binary messages (a client that sends binary is answered in binary); Haxe `Channel` with a `WebSocket` and a `FramedChannel` over sys.net.Socket behind SessionClient. The same conformance checks pass for the session workspace and launcher over both transports against the real server.\n\nOpen, asked of ash-7d: how a guest connect (ip and port, no name resolution) becomes a ws:// or wss:// URL with a host name and path; whether each send is one binary message; how a page adds authentication to the socket (subprotocol or query); whether blocking Socket reads and select suspend a fiber. If the host cannot take a URL, the page needs a small hook (ashPresent-like) naming the session's URL, which ash would add. Browser storage for RayDE's own settings, themes and extensions is separate and tracked under c63a4d7.","files":null}]}